// REVIEW FINDINGS - Project Management Tool, 1 year in production

// CHECK 1 - Signal-based justification: Redux Toolkit was adopted at
// the project's very START, before any genuine signal had manifested,
// 'because the team had used it before on a different project'.
// FINDING: This was premature adoption (exactly lesson 1's warning) -
// however, the application HAS since genuinely grown complex enough
// (task dependencies, multi-step workflows) that Redux is now
// retroactively justified, even though it wasn't initially.

// CHECK 2 - Category assignment: FOUND a genuine mismatch - the
// 'projects' list (fetched from the backend) is stored in a Redux
// slice, with a custom-written refetch-on-focus effect manually
// reimplementing what React Query provides natively.
// FINDING: Migrate 'projects' out of Redux into React Query specifically.

// CHECK 3 - Local/Context scoping: confirmed correct - a single task
// card's hover-preview state correctly remains local useState, never
// lifted into Redux unnecessarily.

// CHECK 4 - Deliberate library choice: the original Redux choice was
// NOT deliberate (per check 1), but given the genuine current
// complexity, sticking with Redux (rather than migrating to Zustand)
// is now justified by its richer DevTools and the team's existing
// familiarity - a legitimate, deliberate decision to make NOW, even
// if the original adoption wasn't deliberate at the time.

// CHECK 5 - Periodic re-evaluation: this is literally the first time
// this kind of review has been conducted in the project's full year
// of existence.
// FINDING: Schedule this 5-check review to recur quarterly going forward.

// VERDICT: 1 genuine migration needed (check 2), 1 retroactive
// justification accepted (check 1), and a new recurring process
// established (check 5) - none of which would have surfaced from
// simply confirming the application currently 'works'.
